From 4861aa06d98a4b1fee6aeee15c4dba6eb2dfd2a3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bmatusiak <bmatusiak@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 19:02:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] okcore: stop the 20 s user timer when a request is decided

okcore_prime_user_confirmation() starts the 20 s user timer
(fadeoffafter20() -> Usertimeout) every time a request is primed, and
nothing stopped it when the request was decided. It ran out 20 s after
the last prime whatever was happening then: if the next OKSIGN/OKDECRYPT
was arriving (its first packet had set pending_operation, its own prime
had not run yet), the old timer answered "Timeout occured while waiting
for confirmation on OnlyKey" for the new request and wiped its packet
details - the host read that as a refusal.

okcore_run_pending_op() runs once a primed request is decided (after the
press or challenge, or at once when no press is needed), so it cancels
the timer there, as the FIDO2 code already does with the same call. A
request that is never decided still times out after 20 s as before.
---
 onlykey/okcore.cpp | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/onlykey/okcore.cpp b/onlykey/okcore.cpp
index 0620dbc..bdd17e1 100644
--- a/onlykey/okcore.cpp
+++ b/onlykey/okcore.cpp
@@ -5542,6 +5542,7 @@ uint8_t okcore_user_input_mode_for_slot(uint8_t slot) {
 // Runs the operation staged by done_process_packets(). CRYPTO_AUTH must
 // already be 4.
 void okcore_run_pending_op() {
+    cancelfadeoffafter20(); /* the request is decided: its 20 s wait for the user is over */
     derived_key_challenge_mode = 0;
     stored_key_challenge_mode = 0;
     if (packet_buffer_details[0] == OKSIGN) {
-- 
